 We should take `Mux1H` seriously by considering its boundary condition. If input signal vector contains only one element, `Mux1H` will always see it as true and return the second parameter. There is another  careless usage of `Mux1H` in [hasti.scala](https://github.com/ucb-bar/rocket-chip/blob/master/src/main/scala/junctions/hasti.scala#L295) as following.
```
// Master muxes (address and data phase are the same)
(masters zip unionGrantMS) foreach { case (m, g) => {
    m.hrdata := Mux1H(g, slaves.map(_.hrdata))
    m.hresp  := Mux1H(g, slaves.map(_.hresp))
} }
```
When there is one slave, all masters will receive the slave's response whether requested the slave  or not. Thankfully, master would ignore that if **m.hready**  is not ready.
```
